WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.520
Film Courage: Robin, for any actor who wants
to be a working actor in Hollywood, what are

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.940
some things that they need to know not just
about the business but also themselves?

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.980
Robin Riker, actor/author: You have to have
a good sense of yourself and a good thick

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:13.980
skin.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.640
It’s very odd that an actor needs to be
able to shed that skin immediately and be

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.820
able to be vulnerable and express feelings.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.349
You must have thick skin because you are not
going to get more jobs that you get.

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:34.770
Even the most famous actors the ones who you
think work all the time don’t get every

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:44.300
job they want and as a working actor (we working
actors) don’t get more jobs than we do.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.980
So you have to know that, you just have to
know that.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.110
And then you have to act.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:57.440
I encourage (alas equity is making this a
little difficult for equity actors but you

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.620
have to perform.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:11.280
So find a class to go to that keeps your instrument
lubricated and working so you know you’re

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:18.390
not going in and trying to read for MacBeth
after not doing anything for six months or

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.180
doing cereal commercials.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.990
So find a workshop some place where you can
work and do scenes and scene study and that

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:27.990
sort of thing.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.080
You have to be very careful of becoming a
professional student.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:38.670
A lot of people come here and take classes
and that satisfies them in a way.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:44.980
Even doing really good work in a class is
not the same as working and so you have to

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:48.201
do that work in order to be able to be good
enough to land work.

00:01:48.201 --> 00:01:49.201
This is not the same as working.

00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:50.201
And so you have to do that work in order to
be able to be good enough to land that work.

00:01:50.201 --> 00:01:54.540
So you have to get on stage as often as you
can.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.360
There are lots of little theaters here that
do plays.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.770
If you’re not equity you can do that.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:07.040
If you are equity you have to do some workarounds
to get that happening now alas.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.980
But get on stage as much as you can so you
are warmed up.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.590
You should always before you go out on an
audition you should be as prepared as you

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:22.300
can possibly be but for yourself (this is
not so much for the audition) but for the

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:30.190
person, go somewhere in public before you
go to the audition.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:38.280
Because you are a human who is trying to display
emotions and if you make this audition the

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.560
most important thing of your day “I’m
getting ready for this.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.240
I can’t do anything before this.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.700
This is it, the focus, the focus, the focus,”
then you get in the room and you’re not

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.010
limber.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:54.770
So I always try to go and have a cup of coffee
or pick up my dry-cleaning or chat with somebody,

00:02:54.770 --> 00:03:00.099
go get something from the market, do something
so I’m limber and I’ve had a conversation

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:03.420
with someone before I’m having my first
conversation with you the casting director

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.420
who hold my future in your hands.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.200
So that’s one thing that I think is very
important and on the flip side of that at

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.760
the end of the audition go somewhere fun,
reward yourself, you know?

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.910
Have something to look forward to because
a lot of times you go in…you can tell, you

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.430
can read the room whether they received you
well or not…you can’t always tell because

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.320
there have been times when I’ve gone in
thinking I’ve stunk up the room and got

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.590
a call a few hours later that I’ve got a
job, so you can’t really judge by that.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:42.250
But if you’ve made the audition or the interview
the most important moment of your day then

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:50.270
you’re very likely to feel this huge letdown
at the end of it or this artificial sort of

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.459
stiffness at the beginning because you haven’t
been a person.

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:56.230
Film Courage: I like that…that’s excellent.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:00.120
Robin: So those I think are the most important
things because everybody has different levels

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.160
of talent so I can’t really speak to that.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:07.810
But those things, you have to understand that
you’re not going to get more jobs than you

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:13.440
do, you have to understand that nobody gets
your job, you get your job.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.159
And never talk about having lost a job when
it was only an audition, you didn’t loose

00:04:18.159 --> 00:04:19.159
anything.

00:04:19.159 --> 00:04:21.939
You can’t loose anything you didn’t have.

00:04:21.939 --> 00:04:27.819
And go out somewhere before the audition to
limber up and have somewhere fun to go after

00:04:27.819 --> 00:04:30.370
the audition to celebrate because you went
to work.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.419
Film Courage: I absolutely love that in terms
of interacting with people and loosing that

00:04:34.419 --> 00:04:36.009
sort of social phobia that we all have.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:39.159
Because we’re all in a hurry and were like
“I don’t have much time and there is lots

00:04:39.159 --> 00:04:42.582
of traffic” and now LA is a little bit more
crowded than it used to be so we are even

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:48.870
more dialed in on directly where we need to
be but go somewhere fun, go to Trader Joe’s

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:49.870
and pick up something.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.159
Robin: Exactly, chat with the guys or girls
or something.

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:55.740
Make something happen…live!

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:01.229
Do something to get yourself person-ized again
instead of focused on the job.

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:02.229
Film Courage: Right.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:04.150
Let’s talk about when you get the job.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:10.860
How should one conduct themselves on set even
if they have a scene where they are only going

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.080
to cross in front of the camera or they have
several lines of dialogue?

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:23.680
Robin: First of all, the crew is usually comprised
of real people who are not full of themselves

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.940
and are willing to share information with
you, you know?

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:31.080
Meet you AD who is going to tell you where
the dressing rooms are and be nice to the

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.099
craft service person, be nice to everyone.

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:45.020
Here is one thing that you must know for sure…if
someone of the regular cast is nice to you

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.379
and reaches out to you, it will be a rarity.

00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:53.550
I am amazed at the number of people who have
every reason to be kind and open to the guest

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:59.659
stars or co-stars who come on the show who
won’t even give them the time of day, just

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:01.949
don’t expect it, don’t expect that.

00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:05.639
You be nice, take care of yourself, you be
kind.

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:10.919
If someone is nice and sweet to you, be nice
and sweet back but don’t try and suddenly

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:18.990
become their best friend because that’s
just going to backfire on you in a major way.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:26.259
Ask questions, feel free to ask questions
but don’t expect that you are going to be

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:33.539
embraced to the company because it doesn’t
happen very often, unless it’s my show because

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:40.569
I make sure I do that because I know how difficult
it can be to be the day player on the set

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:48.210
or the one in for three days or something
and I’ve always made it the point to welcome

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.610
(I think that’s probably the theater thing
again, the pot of chili and the glass of wine

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.520
concept).

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.429
But that’s what they should do, but be on
time, always be on time.

00:06:59.429 --> 00:07:05.801
It’s a good idea to bring a magazine or
book and a comfy pillow with you.

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:11.279
Have a little set bag because you spend a
lot of time waiting and you want to be comfortable

00:07:11.279 --> 00:07:18.379
when you do and most of the trailers and dressing
rooms don’t have a comfy pillow or reading

00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:23.749
material for you to pass the time with.

00:07:23.749 --> 00:07:30.610
If you have any special dietary needs or prohibitions,
make sure you take care of yourself in that

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:31.610
regard.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:39.949
Bring a snack or two that fits those things
and be prepared to wait and check.

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:43.669
If you feel you’ve been waiting a long time
and you’re kind of losing the edge check

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:51.419
with your AD and is it likely “can you give
me an E.T.A.

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:54.959
I’d 15 minutes before you need me, if at
all possible?”

00:07:54.959 --> 00:07:59.339
You may not be able to get that 15 but that’s
why you say “If at all possible?”

00:07:59.339 --> 00:08:07.210
And you can remember I’d love to pump up
for when it’s time to go.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:16.209
And know your lines and be prepared to enjoy
yourself and do a good job.

00:08:16.209 --> 00:08:21.449
Film Courage: I like what you said too about
be friendly but not try to be someone’s

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:25.650
best friend and then on the flip side knowing
that people “Oh!

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:30.240
I can’t wait to work with so-and-so!”
and know that they may not see you and not

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.240
be hurt.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:32.240
Robin: Perfectly said.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.550
Film Courage: Because I think there is the
armor you have to wear and I think you learn

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:39.580
it after a few years of being here but when
you’re new especially you get really…it’s

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:40.640
easy to get hurt.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.890
Or it’s easy to become someone’s best
friend right away and then find out…you

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.020
never hear from them again and you’re shocked!

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:48.020
Robin: Right!

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.020
Right!

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.400
Film Courage: But can you talk about that
armor?

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.390
And it’s not being a mean person, it’s
just being protective.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:59.050
Robin: Yeah…but I also…it’s not being
a mean person.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:06.750
You are more generous than I in that regard,
I find that they are not being a conscious

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.250
person.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.570
If you can say “Hello!

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:15.820
Welcome to the show, nice to have you.”

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:21.540
Now one of the reasons they may not do that
is because when they have done that, the newbie

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.760
has decided “Oh now, she’s my best friend.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.740
I’m going to talk to her, we are going to
have lunch together, whatever.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.980
And that person’s wants “Get off of me!”

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:36.300
Maybe they are protecting themselves because
they have done this before and it kind of

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.360
backfired on them in a way that they didn’t
want to have to deal with.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:40.500
Film Courage: True.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:48.380
Robin: However, it is easy to take a moment
to welcome someone to your show, to be kind

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:53.440
to them, to have a nice word, because we are
all doing it together.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.230
Everyone is in the same boat.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:10:01.960
You have a better seat perhaps than I do if
you’re the regular on the show but we’re

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.110
in the same boat and we’re all actors and
performers.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:12.130
So I find that sort of treatment…I was doing
an episode…and while the make-up lady was

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:17.290
doing her make-up her face was down like this
in her cell phone the whole time.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:21.930
She didn’t speak to anyone, she didn’t
say…now she maybe a perfectly lovely girl.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:26.440
I worked with her for a week, she seemed very
nice on the set.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:33.310
But I use that as an example as how you as
a co-star or a guest star (or any star should

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:38.230
be) on the show is that the people that are
there, the make-up, the hair, they are not

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:42.660
your servants, they are professionals that
are doing their job.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:47.540
Treat them with dignity and respect because
they have control over how you look.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:52.880
You are allowed to say “I prefer to have
my hair blown forward rather than blown back

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.230
or I would like to have it up if possible
blah, blah, blah, you can tell them what it

00:10:56.230 --> 00:11:02.400
is you want you can do it as though you are
speaking to a professional and say please

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.050
and thank you and be a conscious human being.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:10.410
Don’t…you know they are not your staff.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.670
Film Courage: Sure.

